[
  {
    "type": "hero",
    "lede": "Four shapes cover almost every graph on this paper: a straight line, a U, an S-bend and a pair of curves that never touch the axes. The highest power of $x$ decides which one you are looking at, so reading the power is usually the whole question.",
    "can": [
      "Name the family from the highest power of $x$.",
      "Read where a factorised quadratic crosses the $x$-axis.",
      "Draw a reciprocal graph as two branches that never meet an axis."
    ],
    "minutes": 10
  },
  {
    "type": "h",
    "text": "Four shapes, and how to tell them apart"
  },
  {
    "type": "p",
    "md": "Read the highest power first, because it decides everything. Power 1 gives a straight line, power 2 a U-shape, power 3 an S-bend, and an $x$ underneath gives a reciprocal graph in two separate branches. A negative sign in front turns the picture upside down but changes nothing about which family it belongs to."
  },
