    "caption": "One window, four equations. The highest power of x, and whether x is underneath, decides which picture you get."
  },
  {
    "type": "gate",
    "id": "g1",
    "kind": "number",
    "prompt": "One tap to begin. How many separate branches does the graph of $y=\\frac{6}{x}$ have?",
    "answer": "2",
    "explain": "Two: one where $x$ is positive and one where it is negative. There is nothing at $x=0$, so the graph cannot cross from one side to the other."
  },
  {
    "type": "h",
    "text": "The family album"
  },
  {
    "type": "p",
    "md": "Read the highest power of $x$ first, because it decides everything.\n**Power 1** gives a straight line: a constant rate of change, no bend anywhere.\n**Power 2** gives a parabola: one turning point, a U when the $x^{2}$ term is positive and an upside-down U when it is negative.\n**Power 3** gives a cubic: an S shape with up to two turns and up to three crossings of the $x$-axis.\n**Dividing by** $x$ gives the reciprocal: two branches in opposite quadrants, each bending towards the axes but never reaching them."
  },
  {
    "type": "gate",
    "id": "g2",
    "kind": "choice",
    "prompt": "Which family does $y=5-2x^{3}$ belong to?",
    "options": [
      "Cubic",
      "Quadratic",
      "Reciprocal"
    ],
    "answer": "Cubic",
    "explain": "The highest power is 3, so it is a cubic. The minus sign in front flips it over, so the S runs downwards instead of upwards, but the family is unchanged."
  },
  {
    "type": "h",
    "text": "Reading a quadratic without plotting it"
  },
  {
    "type": "p",
    "md": "A quadratic in factorised form has already told you where it crosses the $x$-axis. A product is zero only when one of its factors is zero, so $y=(x+1)(x-5)$ is zero at $x=-1$ and at $x=5$.\nThe $y$-axis crossing needs one substitution: at $x=0$, $y=(1)(-5)=-5$.\nThe turning point comes from symmetry. The curve is a mirror image about the vertical line through its lowest point, so that line sits halfway between the two crossings, at $x=2$. Substituting back gives $y=(3)(-3)=-9$, so the minimum is at $(2, -9)$."
  },
