  {
    "type": "gate",
    "id": "g3",
    "kind": "number",
    "prompt": "The curve $y=(x-1)(x+7)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $1$ and $-7$. What is the $x$-coordinate of its turning point?",
    "answer": "-3",
    "explain": "Halfway between $1$ and $-7$: $\\frac{1+(-7)}{2}=-3$. Substituting would then give the depth, $y=(-4)(4)=-16$."
  },
  {
    "type": "h",
    "text": "Why the reciprocal graph keeps its distance"
  },
  {
    "type": "p",
    "md": "Two facts explain the whole picture. There is no point on the $y$-axis, because a point there would need $x=0$ and zero has no reciprocal. And there is no point on the $x$-axis, because $\\frac{6}{x}$ is never zero however large $x$ grows: at $x=100$ it is $0.06$, at $x=1000$ it is $0.006$, always smaller and never nothing.\nSo each branch runs alongside an axis for ever, closing the gap without ever crossing it. That is why the curve has to be drawn freehand and why it must be carried all the way to the last point in the table."
  },
  {
