  {
    "type": "gate",
    "id": "g4",
    "kind": "choice",
    "prompt": "Your table for $y=\\frac{9}{x}$ includes $x=0.75$. What must your drawn curve do?",
    "options": [
      "Reach the point $(0.75, 12)$",
      "Start at $(1.5, 6)$",
      "Meet the $y$-axis"
    ],
    "answer": "Reach the point $(0.75, 12)$",
    "explain": "Every point in the table is on the graph, and the November 2024 report singled out curves that began at the second point and so did not reach high enough. The curve still never meets the axis itself."
  },
  {
    "type": "callout",
    "kind": "why",
    "title": "Why the highest power decides the shape",
    "md": "Out at the far ends of the grid the highest power grows faster than everything else put together, so it is what the arms of the curve obey. That is why one glance at the power names the family before a single point is plotted."
  },
  {
    "type": "h",
    "text": "See it and try it"
  },
  {
    "type": "video",
    "videoId": "kTTTkMwXqrg",
    "title": "Reciprocal graphs - Corbettmaths",
    "channel": "corbettmaths",
    "corbettmathsNumber": 346,
    "why": "A few minutes on the shape of the reciprocal graph and how the table is built. Watch it once, then answer the gate below without going back."
  },
  {
    "type": "gate",
    "id": "g5",
    "kind": "choice",
    "prompt": "A curve is in two branches, one in the top right and one in the bottom left. Which equation could it have?",
    "options": [
      "$y=\\frac{4}{x}$",
      "$y=4x$",
      "$y=x^{2}+4$"
    ],
    "answer": "$y=\\frac{4}{x}$",
    "explain": "Only dividing by $x$ splits a graph in two. With a positive numerator the branches sit in the top right and bottom left; a negative one would put them in the other pair of quadrants."
  },
  {
    "type": "sim",
    "provider": "phet",
    "url": "https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/graphing-quadratics/latest/graphing-quadratics_en.html",
    "title": "PhET: Graphing Quadratics",
    "attribution": "Simulation by PhET Interactive Simulations, University of Colorado Boulder, licensed under CC BY-NC 4.0 (https://phet.colorado.edu)",
    "licence": "CC BY-NC 4.0",
    "task": "Open the Explore screen and set the equation to y = x squared. Make the number in front of x squared negative and watch the curve turn over. Then change the constant on the end and watch the whole curve slide up and down without changing shape. Two things to take away: the sign in front of x squared decides U or upside-down U, and the constant is the y-axis crossing."
  },
  {
    "type": "gate",
    "id": "g6",
    "kind": "choice",
    "prompt": "In the simulation you made the number in front of $x^{2}$ negative. What happened to the turning point?",
    "options": [
      "It became a maximum instead of a minimum",
      "It moved onto the $y$-axis",
      "It disappeared"
    ],
    "answer": "It became a maximum instead of a minimum",
    "explain": "A negative $x^{2}$ term opens the curve downwards, so the turning point becomes the highest point rather than the lowest. Its position along the $x$-axis is unchanged."
  },
  {
    "type": "h",
    "text": "Where the marks are actually lost"
  },
  {
    "type": "callout",
    "kind": "examiner",
    "title": "November 2024, M7 Paper 1 Q16",
    "md": "Draw a reciprocal graph from a table, then read one value off it. Few candidates collected all three marks for the drawing. Many drew a straight line. Others plotted every point correctly but began the curve at the first whole-number point, leaving out the tabulated point nearer the $y$-axis, so the curve did not climb far enough for the last mark.",
    "source": "ccea-cer:maths:2024-november:M71:Q16"
  },
  {
    "type": "gate",
    "id": "g7",
    "kind": "blank",
    "prompt": "You have plotted every point of a curve. What do you join them with?",
    "answer": "a smooth curve | smooth curve | freehand | a freehand curve | one smooth curve | pencil freehand",
    "explain": "One smooth freehand curve through every point. A ruler costs the curve mark in every series it is reported, and so does running flat between the two lowest points."
  },
  {
    "type": "callout",
    "kind": "mustknow",
    "title": "Sheet or memory?",
    "md": "**On the M7 and M8 formula sheet (page 2):** nothing for this topic.\n**Must be known:** the four shapes and the power that produces each; that the sign of the $x^{2}$ term decides U or upside-down U; that the crossings come from the brackets and the $y$-intercept from $x=0$; that the turning point is halfway between the crossings; and that $y=\\frac{k}{x}$ touches neither axis."
  },
  {
    "type": "gate",
    "id": "g8",
    "kind": "choice",
    "prompt": "A question says: use your graph to estimate $y$ when $x=2.6$. You would rather substitute into the equation. What happens?",
    "options": [
      "No mark: the question asked for a reading",
      "Full marks, because the value is right",
      "Half the mark"
    ],
    "answer": "No mark: the question asked for a reading",
    "explain": "The November 2025 report recorded exactly this. The mark is for reading the graph, so draw the lines up and across and write down what you see; you can always check against the equation afterwards."
  },
